# 0068-remove-game-file-after-build.patch -- after a SUCCESSFUL build the game file of a game the app imported itself is replaced by an empty stub of the same name; the game stays in the library; every import is recorded in Documents/gameapp.imports.tsv.
# Applies after 0067 (the tree with 0010..0067 applied). UNTESTED ON DEVICE: compiled (dotnet build, ios-arm64, Release: rc 0, no error, 39 warnings: the same as the 0067 tree, none new) and the pure classes run in a host harness (a net console program built
# from the real source text of GameAppSourceRemoval.cs and the classes it uses: 341 checks in all, 88 of them new here, 52 mutations of the classes in all, 17 of them new here, each caught); no iPhone run. Measured and assumed are told apart below.
#
# WHY (the owner's decision): an imported game is a clone or a copy of a file that exists somewhere else ("since the game is cloned it is already somewhere"), and after the build iBuilder needs its build, not the file. The library is made from the game files in
#   Documents (Dumps()), so deleting the file would make a built game vanish; it is replaced by a ZERO-BYTE STUB with the same name: the game keeps its name, title id, cover, settings, saves, mods, its Ready status (the stamp), Play and Export IPA (all of which
#   go by name or by the build, not by the file's content).
#
# THE RECORD -- GameAppSourceRemoval.cs (new; no UIKit, run by the harness): ImportRecord / ImportLedger = Documents/gameapp.imports.tsv, one line per file the APP imported: name, size, how (clone | copy | download), source (a short label: "picker", "folder <name>",
#   the Cloud page's source name, "NAS"), source id (the Cloud source's id when there is one), time, and the time its content was removed ("" = still here). Written through a .part and one rename under one lock (imports run on workers), read again only when the file
#   changed, never throws on a bad file (an unreadable ledger is an empty one: nothing is removed). The launch sweep (0063) knows gameapp.imports.tsv.part. Every import path records:
#     * the old Import picker (CopyIn: iOS copied the file into tmp/, the app moved it): how=copy, source "picker"; written on a worker;
#     * "Add from a folder without copying" (0067): how=clone or copy from GameImport.Place, source "folder <name>"; a file that was "AlreadyThere" is not recorded;
#     * the Cloud page's Download and Download and build: a Files-folder source: how=clone or copy (CopyFromFolder now returns the Placed), source = the source's name and id; the NAS: how=download, source "NAS";
#     * "Find them in a folder" (an update or DLC found in a folder, CopyContentIn): how=copy, source "folder <name>".
#   Only game files (.nsp .xci .nsz .nup) are recorded: never prod.keys. A file the owner put into Documents by hand has NO record and is never removed. Deleting a game removes its records (PerformDelete's worker).
#
# WHEN (SourceRemoval.ShouldRemove, one file at a time; Plan = the game's base file, its update and its DLC, each by its own record): a file is replaced ONLY IF every line holds: it is a game file (never prod.keys, a certificate, anything else) / the Settings
#   toggle is on / the build's chain returned 0 (StopOutcome.Finished: not a pause, a cancel or a failure; an OS kill never reaches it; the hook is in BeginRun's chain-return callback after Finish(), for a build, not a boot) / the framework stamp names this game
#   and the update / DLC in Documents are what the build included (the stamp's content= signature; an update or DLC added since the build stays) / no build of this game runs, is paused, or waits in the queue / the ledger says THE APP imported the file and it is
#   not removed yet / the file is still what was imported (its size is the record's: a file the owner replaced by hand is not ours) and has content. A "removed" record on a file that has content again (put back by hand) is NOT ours either. The removal is on a worker
#   thread, never the main thread: `source: removed <name> (<size>) after the build (it was cloned|copied|downloaded, from <source>)` per file, and ONCE, before the very first removal ever, `source: from now on a built game's file is replaced by an empty stub of the
#   same name, the game stays in the library (Settings > Remove the game file after a successful build turns this off)`. A file kept for a reason worth saying gets a `source: <file> kept (...)` line (a hand-placed file does not).
#   Execute: the LEDGER FIRST (the record says "removed"), then the stub (SourceStub.Replace: <name>.part written empty and renamed over the file: one rename, so a reader sees the old file or the stub, never half of either); if the stub cannot be made the record is put
#   back and the file is as it was; if the ledger cannot be written nothing is touched.
#
# WHAT A STUB MEANS TO THE REST OF THE APP
#   * IsStub(size, record) = size 0 and a "removed" record (a zero-byte file with no record is "empty": the same guards, the word "Empty file"). Never a game with 0 bytes shown as such: the size column of the tile / row chip, the Build tab row, the game sheet's Game
#     line and the build screen say "File removed".
#   * What needs the CONTENT asks first (GameFileHasContent: the base game, its update and its DLC must all have content): Set up (SetUpAndPlay, so also "Set up and play", Build, Update, Resume from the start and every flow that calls it), Rebuild (the alert is not
#     even shown), Update (adding an update or DLC after the build), "Add to the build queue", and the queue's own start (no alert in the middle of a queue: the item fails with "the game file was removed after the build: add it again", the queue goes on). Message:
#     "The game file was removed after the build to free space. Add it again to rebuild." (title "The game file was removed") with "Add it again from <source>" when the Files-folder source the file came from still lists it (one button adds back every missing file that
#     source lists: Download clones them over the stubs, instant), "Add a game file…" (the old picker), "Add from a folder…" (0067), "Not now". The icon passes (FetchMissingIcons, FetchMissingNupIcons) skip a file with no content. Play, Export IPA, the sheet, cover,
#     title id, settings, saves, mods keep working: they never read the file.
#   * Importing a file with the same name over a stub REPLACES it: "Add from a folder" does not count a stub as "In the library" (0067's check), the old picker's CopyIn and Download replace by rename; the new import writes a new record (not removed).
#
# THE SETTING: Settings > BUILD, a switch in the flat list: "Remove the game file after a successful build" (id setting-remove-source; default ON: the owner asked for it; key remove_source_after_build in gameapp.settings.tsv; off = nothing is removed). Its note:
#   "iBuilder imported the file as a clone or a copy of one that is somewhere else: once the game is built it keeps the game (its name, cover, settings and saves) and frees the file. Add the file again to rebuild. Files you put into iBuilder's folder yourself are
#   never removed." The exported player has none of this.
#
# NEW STRINGS: "Remove the game file after a successful build", the note above, "File removed", "Empty file", "The game file was removed", "The game file was removed after the build to free space. Add it again to rebuild.", "Add it again from <source>", "Add a game file…",
#   "Add from a folder…", "Not now", "The game file is empty", "The game file has no content. Add it again to build it.", "the game file was removed after the build: add it again" (a failed queue item's note). Log lines: `source: removed <name> (<size>) after the build (it was <how>, from
#   <source>)`, `source: from now on a built game's file is replaced by an empty stub ...`, `source: <file> kept (...)`, `source: <game>: the game file was removed after the build (<files>): nothing to build from`, `source: adding <files> again from <source>`, `source: the import of <name>
#   could not be recorded (...)`, `build setting: the game file is removed (an empty stub stays) after a successful build of a game iBuilder imported` / `... is kept after a build`. ACCESSIBILITY IDS: setting-remove-source (the switch).
#
# MEASURED (host harness, .NET 11, Apple silicon Mac, APFS): the ledger (format / parse round trip, garbage, a name twice in any case, tabs and newlines in a field, 160 imports from 16 threads: 160 records and a whole file, no .part, an unreadable ledger is empty, an
#   unwritable one throws and leaves no .part); ShouldRemove over a table of 23 cases and 30,720 generated cases against an independent statement of the rule (Remove exactly when every line holds: 4 of them); the plans (base + update + DLC, each by its own record:
#   an imported update and DLC go although the base was put there by hand, and the other way round; a DLC added since the build: nothing of the game goes; a failed build, a running build: nothing); the stub (the content is gone, same name, no .part; the rename
#   fails: the game file is exactly as it was; 300 replacements under a reader (over 50,000 reads in every run): the file was never missing and never anything but its full size or empty); EXECUTED on real folder trees: the base game, its update and its imported DLC become 0-byte stubs
#   with the log lines above, the DLC put there by hand, Beta (by hand) and another imported game are untouched, keys / certificate / saves / cover / shader cache / extract / Exports / the stamp keep their SHA-256, run again removes nothing; a failed, paused or
#   cancelled build, the toggle off, a build of the game running: the same tree untouched; a stub that cannot be made for one file: that file keeps its content and record, the others go; a ledger that cannot be written: the file is not touched; an import over a stub
#   replaces it (a real clone). SourceReAdd and the words.
# NOT EXERCISED / ASSUMED (no device run): everything UIKit (the switch, the alert, the size texts, the queue's failed note); that BeginRun's chain-return callback is reached with rc 0 after a real build (and that a build finishing in a later launch, after the
#   stage-3 restart, reaches it too: it is the same callback); that the stamp's game= is the file name the library uses (the harness assumes it, as GameStatusOf does); atomic rename and the stub on the phone's APFS; a re-add through "Add it again from <source>" on the
#   phone (Download clones over the stub); the first removal's one-time line on a real ledger; the storage bars after a removal.
# RISKS: a file the owner replaces by hand with another of EXACTLY the same size would be taken for the imported one (the record has no hash: reading 26 GB would defeat the purpose); the ledger is written on a worker after CopyIn, so an app kill in that instant leaves no
#   record (the file is then never removed: the safe direction); a "removed" record whose stub was never made (a kill between the two) leaves a file with content under a "removed" record: it is treated as put there by hand and never removed; records of files the owner
#   deletes in Files stay (harmless: the file is not there); the toggle is global, not per game.
